bsqrt-order.patch -- Fase 2 scheduling: t1-before-t2 for bSqrt (SN-F2).

With -ffast-math + -fschedule-insns/-fschedule-insns2, each Newton step comes
out as t0, t2, t1, fmadds: after t0, both t1 (y0*half, independent) and t2
(one-x*t0) are ready, and haifa lets t2 jump ahead of t1, while the original
(asm) keeps t1 first (FEngSqrt 82.08% with plain C+builtins in Fase 1).
No C formulation fixes it (five source orders all come out t2-first with the
game flags); the first scheduler alone preserves source order, so scheduling
is the culprit.  ADJUST_PRIORITY cannot fix the second pass: haifa gates it
to pre-reload, and ADJUST_COST results are clamped to >= 1.

What it does: one narrow MD_SCHED_REORDER hook for the Fase 1 pattern
(b_fnmsubs_minus_sf2, now named so CODE_FOR exists): after every ready-list
sort, in both passes, demote those fnmsubs below every other ready insn
(stable partition; haifa issues from the end), so t1 issues first.
Dependencies still pin t2 before its fmadds; only this pattern is touched,
no flag, cost-table or type change.  Requires the Fase 1 rename in
fnmsubs-minus.patch; build.sh applies it in both source toolchains after
fnmsubs-minus.

--- a/gcc/config/rs6000/rs6000.c
+++ b/gcc/config/rs6000/rs6000.c
@@ -5773,6 +5773,43 @@
   return priority;
 }
 
+/* SN-F2 (bSqrt order t1-before-t2): ready-list tweak for the new `c-a*b'
+   fnmsubs (b_fnmsubs_minus_sf2).  In both scheduling passes, after t0 an
+   independent fmuls (t1 = y0*half) and this fnmsubs (t2 = one-x*t0) are
+   ready together, and haifa's tie-breaks (register weight pre-reload,
+   LUID order post-reload) let t2 jump ahead of t1; the original keeps t1
+   first (FEngSqrt 82.08% with plain C+builtins in Fase 1).  Unlike
+   ADJUST_PRIORITY, which haifa gates to pre-reload, MD_SCHED_REORDER runs
+   in both passes: stable-partition READY so these fnmsubs come first
+   (= least preferred; haifa issues from the end), preserving relative
+   order otherwise.  Dependencies still pin t2 before its fmadds; only
+   this pattern is touched.  */
+void
+rs6000_sched_reorder (ready, n_ready)
+     rtx *ready;
+     int n_ready;
+{
+  int i, j;
+  rtx tmp;
+
+  for (i = 1; i < n_ready; i++)
+    {
+      tmp = ready[i];
+      if (GET_RTX_CLASS (GET_CODE (tmp)) != 'i'
+          || recog_memoized (tmp) != CODE_FOR_b_fnmsubs_minus_sf2)
+        continue;
+      for (j = i; j > 0; j--)
+        {
+          rtx prev = ready[j - 1];
+          if (GET_RTX_CLASS (GET_CODE (prev)) == 'i'
+              && recog_memoized (prev) == CODE_FOR_b_fnmsubs_minus_sf2)
+            break;
+          ready[j] = prev;
+        }
+      ready[j] = tmp;
+    }
+}
+
 /* Return how many instructions the machine can issue per cycle */
 int get_issue_rate()
 {
--- a/gcc/config/rs6000/rs6000.h
+++ b/gcc/config/rs6000/rs6000.h
@@ -1068,6 +1068,13 @@
 #define ADJUST_PRIORITY(INSN)						\
   INSN_PRIORITY (INSN) = rs6000_adjust_priority (INSN, INSN_PRIORITY (INSN))
 
+/* SN-F2 (bSqrt order t1-before-t2): ready-list tweak in both scheduling
+   passes (ADJUST_PRIORITY above only runs pre-reload).  See
+   rs6000_sched_reorder in rs6000.c.  */
+extern void rs6000_sched_reorder ();
+#define MD_SCHED_REORDER(DUMP, SCHED_VERBOSE, READY, N_READY)		\
+  rs6000_sched_reorder (READY, N_READY)
+
 /* Define this macro to change register usage conditional on target flags.
    Set MQ register fixed (already call_used) if not POWER architecture
    (RIOS1, RIOS2, RSC, and PPC601) so that it will not be allocated.
